tails of  that person's life that's not to say  that a biographer has to like her  subject although I do think it helps but  a biographer has to have some common  interest some personal stake other than  purely financial or professional one in  exploring someone else's life my  connection to Laura Ingalls Wilder like  so many other Wilder fans and like so  many of the  students I met today when I talked  briefly to a group who was studying  Laura Ingalls Wilder my connection with  her began very early at about the same  time I read Narciso Whitman's biography  I read my first little house novel in  addition to reading biographies and  catching perch with my grandfather  that's me in the middle I like to read  historical fiction another literary  staple for young readers growing up in  the 1940s 50s and 60s I was 10 years old  when I read my first little house book  and the following spring on May 29th  1965 I visited Laura Ingalls Wilder's  home at Rocky Ridge Farm in the Missouri  Ozarks for the first time I had just  recently turned 11 years old how can I  be so sure of that date May 29th 1965  because I wrote about it in this very  diary the first one I ever kept and for  that day I wrote went to Wilder home I  kept the entry short because I was  writing in a five-year diary  and I had only four lines to describe  the entire day but don't let the brevity  of an eleven-year-old fool you  I was completely inspired on that May  Day back in 1965 I'd learned something  that would stay with me throughout my  childhood and adolescent I learned that  someone from the Missouri Ozarks could  publish a book or as my father might say  even a hillbilly can write after all  Wilders farm was just 40 miles from  where I grew up in Springfield Missouri  and Wilder had been a farmer's wife  before she published her first book I  mean look at her in this picture she's  sitting at her dining room table  surrounded by her books wearing an  ordinary house dress just like the ones  my 