 of five of Wilders nine books I  was well on my way toward a riding  career I'd already been a staff writer  at my hometown newspaper where I'd  worked with a photographer who had taken  many years before that picture of Laura  Ingalls Wilder  in the house dress at her dining room  table I was soon to freelance for the  Sioux City Journal and to write for  state agencies in South Dakota what I  hadn't bargained for however when I  moved to South Dakota was all that big  empty sky this is what I'd been used to  rocky hills low hazy skies the lush  Bluegreen of Missouri when Laura Ingalls  Wilder first saw the Missouri Ozarks in  1894 when she Almanzo and their daughter  Rose first moved to Missouri from South  Dakota  it was the contrast in sky that caught  her eye the sky seems lower here and it  is the softest blue the distances and  the valleys are blue where ever you can  see them it is a drowsy country that  makes you feel wide awake and alive but  somehow contented in 1974 when I moved  from Missouri to South Dakota I  exchanged that soft blue drowsy sky for  this the land stretching flat and almost  treeless in every direction it made me  feel small vulnerable and exposed on my  first day in South Dakota I said on the  curb in front of the big dilapidated old  Victorian house butchered in two  apartments for impoverished college  students and wept whatever had possessed  me to move west  over the next few months I reread Wilder  South Dakota novels her books worked  their magic I embraced the big prairie  sky the alien landscape and discovered  its subtle but somehow dramatic beauty  yet Wilders books did more than heal the  wounds of moving West they began to  teach me about the writing craft and I  came away with a new respect for Wilder  not just as a writer of children's books  but as a writer with a capital W I began  to write professionally about Laura  Ingalls Wilder in the 1970s when I  worked for the South Dakota Division of  Tourism and I met old-timers in De Smet  South Dakota who had known the Ingalls  family and even Laura herself Here I am  at my tourism desk where I wrote many of  these stories and if you look closely  very closely you'll see a photograph of  the Ingalls girls just below the  bulletin board on the right side of the  photograph I kept all of those interview  notes and materials from these stories  as I moved west across the country from  South Dakota to Kansas to Colorado and  finally to Oregon where Charles Ingalls  himself had always hoped to settle I  also began to write fiction for young  adults my fiction is very different than  Wilders little house books but like her  I was first a journalist more about this  in a minute and eventually a novelist so  because of my personal connection I felt  with Wilder and her work I was  immediately attracted to the idea of  writing a biography when the South  Dakota State Historical Society press  first contacted me about it but I knew  the wilder myth and it read the previous  biographies about her and so I hesitated  would I have anything new to say about  her hadn't everything already been said  one more autobiographical note my late  husband who understood Wilders influence  on my childhood and my professional  encouraged me to take on the project  when the offer to write the book came he  was battling stage 4 cancer and while I  was grappling with whether to say yes to  the project we learned his cancer was  unstoppable you'll need this book when  I'm gone he told me during the last  weeks of his life which brings me back  to this idea biographers should feel a  personal connection to their subjects I  said yes to writing a biography of Laura  Ingalls Wilder for myself for my husband  and for all that Laura Ingalls Wilder  had met to me over my lifetime little  did I know however when I said yes just  how important it would be to have a  personal connection with my subject what  I didn't know then but I do know now is  that a biographer subje